WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.365
Why do this by hand.

2
00:00:01.645 --> 00:00:03.971
PyTorch computes every gradient for you.

3
00:00:04.251 --> 00:00:23.600
The lecture’s opening argument (00:00:00 to 07:26) is that this convenience is a leaky abstraction: when training misbehaves, the cause is usually in how gradients flow through some operation, and you can only reason about that if you can compute it yourself.

4
00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:30.877
It also recounts how hand-written backward passes were simply normal practice in the field until fairly recently.

5
00:00:31.157 --> 00:00:34.080
For you, the payoff is different and very concrete.

6
00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:36.749
Lesson 1 gave you backprop on scalars.

7
00:00:37.029 --> 00:00:42.704
Real models do it on tensors, where a single node is a matrix multiply over a batch.

8
00:00:42.984 --> 00:00:52.840
Working out those tensor-level rules once, carefully, means that when you read a paper’s update rule or a fine-tuning library’s custom kernel, the math is legible.

9
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.757
And the method you’ll use, index notation plus a shape check plus a gradient check, works for any operation anyone invents.

10
00:01:01.037 --> 00:01:05.667
The video description is blunt that this lecture is not meant to be simply watched.

11
00:01:05.947 --> 00:01:10.555
Do the exercise in the linked notebook alongside it, pausing before each answer.

12
00:01:10.835 --> 00:01:14.141
The notes below give the derivations so you can check your work.
